    "summary": "Pigpen‑driven jam marathon with monster Alligator solo",
    "blurb": "The show is built around a ferocious Alligator jam and extended Pigpen‑heavy improvisations. Highlights include a scorching Jerry solo, soulful Good Lovin', and a raw Turn On Your Love Light despite tape hiss. Overall it’s a solid snapshot of the Dead’s late‑’69 sound.",
    "review": "On November 1, 1969 at San Francisco’s Family Dog, the Grateful Dead delivered a loose‑loving set that leans heavily on Ron \"Pigpen\" McKernan’s bluesy presence and a towering Alligator jam. Jerry ignites the Alligator segment with a fiery solo that rides over the dual drum groove and gongs, while Tom Constanten weaves quirky organ textures underneath. The band rolls through Good Lovin' and a gritty Turn On Your Love Light (the latter suffers a brief cut but remains compelling), and the Jam after drums showcases Pigpen’s harmonica grit and the group’s willingness to stretch out. Though the soundboard capture is marred by tape hiss and a muddy low‑end typical of 1969 tapes, the performance energy shines through, making it an essential listen for fans of the band’s early improvisational era.",
